====== Installation ======

===== SD Card =====

  - Prepare SD Card and install OpenWrt Image on it _or_ see tftp Installation
  - set DIP Switch to boot from "SD/eMMC"
  - connect Serial Console and Power-On

Note: The U-Boot Loader is already included in the SD Card Image.

Extract the OpenWrt Image and write it to the SD Card and existing data will be overwritten.
Be patient and use correct device to write data.

<code> # zcat <image>-sdcard.img.gz | dd of=/dev/mmcblkX bs=4k conv=sync status=progress</code>

===== M.2 SATA SSD =====

  - Prepare M.2 SATA SSD and install OpenWrt Image on it _or_ see tftp Installation
  - set DIP Switch to boot from "M.2 SATA SSD"
  - connect Serial Console and Power-On

Note: The U-Boot Loader is already included in the SD Card Image.

Extract the OpenWrt Image and write it to the SD Card and existing data will be overwritten.
Be patient and use correct device to write data.

<code> # zcat <image>-sdcard.img.gz | dd of=/dev/sdX bs=4k conv=sync status=progress</code>

===== eMMC =====

  - see tftp Installation
  - set DIP Switch to boot from "SD/eMMC"
  - connect Serial Console and Power-On

===== TFTP =====

  - Setup a tftp Server and store "solidrun_clearfog-base-a1-initramfs-kernel.bin" or "solidrun_clearfog-pro-a1-initramfs-kernel.bin". see: [[:docs:guide-user:installation:generic.flashing.tftp]]
  - Use UART Boot Mode to boot initial U-Boot _or_ use SPI Boot Mode for factory installed U-Boot
  - connect Serial Console and get a U-Boot prompt
  - eMMC only: configure boot device
  - configure tftp client settings on U-Boot
  - load and boot OpenWrt "initramfs" Image

==== eMMC only - configure boot partition ====

The eMMC itself must also be configured for selecting the correct boot partition. \\
Responsible for changing this setting is the mmc partconf command. It takes either 1, or 4 paramaters:

**print configuration of mmc 0**
<code>
> mmc partconf 0
BOOT_ACK: 0x0
BOOT_PARTITION_ACCESS: 0x0
PARTITION_ACCESS: 0x0
</code>

The most relevant piece here is the BOOT_PARTITION_ACCESS field. It takes one of the following values:

    0 : do not boot from eMMC
    1 : boot from boot0
    2 : boot from boot1
    7 : boot from data partition 

PARTITION_ACCESS is supposed to control access to the boot partitions where 0 means no access, 1 means read-write for boot0 and 2 read-write for boot1. However this currently does not appear to have any effect. It is suggested to set this to 1 when booting from boot0, 2 when booting from boot1 and 0 when booting from the data partition.

So finally this is how a new configuration is applied:

**configure mmc 0 to boot from, and enable access to, boot0 (/dev/mmcblk0boot0)**
<code>
> mmc partconf 0 0 1 1
</code>
**configure mmc 0 to boot from, and enable access to, boot1 (/dev/mmcblk0boot1)**
<code>
> mmc partconf 0 0 2 2
</code>
**configure mmc 0 to boot from the data partition, and disable access to boot partitions (/dev/mmcblk0)**
<code>
> mmc partconf 0 0 7 0
</code>

